WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.701
-Our guest tonight hosts
the Emmy- and Peabody-winning

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:06.434
show "Last Week Tonight,"
which airs Sunday nights on HBO

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:07.968
and streams on HBO Max.

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:10.701
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend, our good friend,

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:11.701
John Oliver.

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:14.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:22.300
♪♪

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:29.868
♪♪

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:31.334
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:32.434
-I wanted to make it clear.

00:00:32.434 --> 00:00:36.133
-Yeah, that was good.
My stomach's --

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:37.300
Stop them. Stop them.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.968
-You stop it!
-Stop it.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:40.567
-This --
-You have the power.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.434
-Let me explain
something to you.

00:00:41.434 --> 00:00:42.968
-You have the power.

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:45.901
-What you're doing right now is
Kryptonite to this British man.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:47.167
-That's right, he doesn't --
I do --

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:48.834
-He does not want your praise
or attention.

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:51.934
-I do not thrive on that.
I'm diminished by it.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:53.200
Yeah, thank you
for the correction.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.634
-Yeah.
-Good friend, not friend.

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.267
I will be listening so carefully
when you introduce someone

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.000
as "friend" in the future
and leave it there.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.701
-Yeah. I'm just going to be
real dead.

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:02.801
I'm, "Please welcome
our friend."

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.400
-[ Laughs ]
-And that's it.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.634
-That's right.

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:07.801
-Interestingly, the last
couple times you were here

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.067
have been big days
for the royal family news.

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.801
Last time you were here was
the funeral of the Queen.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:15.167
And the time before that --
-Thank you for that.

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:17.133
-You're very welcome.
-Thank you, yeah.

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:18.634
-It was a couple days after
Prince Philip died.

00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:21.100
-Thank you, thank you.
-You're very welcome.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.801
-Wow. You're coming at me with
a lot there.

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:26.634
-We kind of have a vacuum of
royal family news right now.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:28.734
-Well, we have a vacuum
as we not --

00:01:28.734 --> 00:01:30.501
I mean, you tape hours
before broadcast.

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.133
-That's true.
-We don't know what's going

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.901
to happen between now
and this show goes out.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:36.667
Maybe Charles has thrown himself
off the roof

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:40.267
of Buckingham Palace, saying,
"My family is built on blood."

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:41.901
It would be terrible.
It would be terrible.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.200
I would respect it,
but it would be terrible.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.767
-Okay.
-Also, before the royal deaths,

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:50.167
before natural selection
went 2 for 2.

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:51.567
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.634
-The time before that I was here
and COVID was breaking out.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:56.234
-Yes, that's true.

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:58.200
-We do tend -- it's not like
there's going to be a run

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.200
on the banks, right?
-Yeah.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.033
-Right, Seth?
-You're sort of a news reaper.

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:06.667
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:08.434
-Or a crisis fluffer.
-Yeah.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:11.067
You're a crisis fluffer. You
make us feel a little better.

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:12.834
-I'm sure the banks
are going to be fine.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:14.434
That segment was great.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:15.601
-Well, thank you very much.

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.567
I mean, if they weren't going
to be fine,

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:18.968
I think that segment fixed it.

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:21.434
-I think what we all need,
more than anything right now,

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:24.200
is for Jim Cramer to shut
the [bleep] up.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.968
He's in this building, right?

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:27.100
-He might be in this building.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.734
You can sometimes hear him based
on -- yeah.

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:30.767
-Someone in this building
needs to take

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:32.734
a tranquillizer dart
and just [blows]

00:02:32.734 --> 00:02:35.200
get that right.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.667
-Although he does feel like
one of those gorillas

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.868
that can actually --
-Fight through it.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.901
-Throw it down.
-Throw it back at you.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:45.601
-"Buy! Buy Chase Bank.
Everything's gonna be fine!"

00:02:45.601 --> 00:02:48.133
Oh, no, Jim, not like this.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:49.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.300
-You were counterprogrammed
last night

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.100
with a pretty big event.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.167
The Oscars were your competition
last night.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:56.901
-Yeah, that's right.
-Yeah.

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:58.701
-I mean, you're stretching
the definition of the term

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:01.400
"competition" there.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.033
Oscars is one of the most
glamorous nights that humanity

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:05.434
produces each year.

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.767
We counterprogram that with
talking about the temporary

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:10.334
assistance for needy families
program for 22 minutes.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:12.300
yeah, we were the opposite
of the Oscars.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.501
-And you didn't have
a red carpet last night?

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:16.033
I can't remember.
-I mean, we did.

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:19.467
But it didn't get any
of the attention, yeah.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:22.133
-I thought our colleague,
Jimmy Kimmel,

00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:23.667
did a fantastic job last night.
-He did great.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:25.901
Yeah, I watched him, and then --
Yeah, awesome.

00:03:25.901 --> 00:03:27.400
He's great at it.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.434
-Would you ever want to
host an event like that?

00:03:29.434 --> 00:03:32.100
-No. And I don't -- It's not
even that I wouldn't want to.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.234
I don't think it would be a net
benefit for anyone involved.

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.234
'Cause the great thing
about Kimmel, right,

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:40.334
is that he is obviously funny,
but also, he has, like --

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:43.434
you're confident that there
is this low-lying contempt

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:45.167
for the whole event.
-Yep.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:47.501
-And you're confident that
it's there,

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:50.801
but he somehow doesn't manage
to poison the room.

00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:53.100
-Yes.
-With that visible contempt.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.968
And that is a kind of
magic trick I couldn't do.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.968
I could not at any point say,

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.300
"It's going to be
a magical night."

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.200
I'd say, "It's not, though,
is it?"

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.601
These are some of the most
over-praised people on Earth,

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:06.267
and we're going to give them
shiny trophies

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:07.367
like they're dogs.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:09.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:09.968
-Yeah. I couldn't --

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:12.701
-It's not me. No one wants it.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:15.734
-I couldn't ever imagine
you saying anything is gonna be

00:04:15.734 --> 00:04:18.701
magical without you being ironic
or sarcastic.

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.400
-I couldn't do it.
I tried.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.834
Sometimes even at benefit gigs.

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:24.334
-Yeah, we do charitable
events.

00:04:24.334 --> 00:04:26.200
-Yeah, you're good at them.
-Oh, that's very kind.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.133
-You have the Kimmel skill of
not ruining everyone's evening.

00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:32.667
I really struggle with that.

00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:35.400
The only one I can kind of do
functionally

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.000
is the "Sesame Street" Gala
because they're nice, right?

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.734
So they need a bad guy.

00:04:40.734 --> 00:04:42.400
They need a bad cop,

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.400
and I'm willing to be
Elmo's bad cop.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.167
So they can say, "Oh, we're all
here to have fun."

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:49.334
And I can say, "We're not,
actually, Elmo.

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:51.901
We're here to squeeze that table
of bankers at Goldman Sachs

00:04:51.901 --> 00:04:54.400
for more money than
they're willing to spend.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.200
Go away.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.901
Don't be here when
the transaction happens, Elmo.

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:00.267
Mafia rules only here."

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:02.767
-Do you feel like you
have a good working relationship

00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:05.434
with the Muppets now, based on
your time at those benefits?

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:06.868
-Yeah, actually. Not bad.

00:05:06.868 --> 00:05:08.834
Like, I really love them.

00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:12.601
The first time I met one, and I
still can't really think of it

00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:15.400
as the puppeteer, I did
something with Cookie Monster.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.601
And he was so funny.

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:21.133
And I had heard that one of the
old writers of "The Daily Show,"

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:23.100
Rich Blomquist,
had met Fozzy Bear once

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.834
when Kristen Schaal
was in the Muppet movie.

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:28.501
And he'd said he wanted to meet
Fozzy Bear.

00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:31.634
And so he's talking to him
and he just instantly forgets

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:33.467
there's a guy underneath him
and starts saying to him,

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.167
"I think you're the reason
I got into comedy, Fozzy."

00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:37.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:39.501
-And you can feel the puppeteer,
he said he could feel him go,

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.033
"Oh, [bleep], this is one
of those conversations.

00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:43.601
Okay. Oh.

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:46.934
You're welcome, waka waka waka."

00:05:46.934 --> 00:05:49.767
But he said, he just had this
very sincere monologue

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.400
about, "I think you
fundamentally changed

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.634
the way my life has gone."

00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:56.667
-I did a Weekend Update
feature once with Kermit.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:59.067
And one of my favorite photos
at my time at "SNL,"

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:03.033
is -- it was during rehearsal,
and I'm just looking --

00:06:03.033 --> 00:06:04.501
I'm giving a note to Kermit.

00:06:04.501 --> 00:06:06.167
And it's just this picture of me
talking to him

00:06:06.167 --> 00:06:08.968
like he's listening.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:11.701
-And I know the dude is right
there, and I'm like --

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:12.767
-It's incredible.
-Yeah.

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:14.133
-That is how amazing they are.

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:15.434
I was doing that whole thing
with Cookie Monster.

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:17.834
I never once did I look
that poor guy in the face.

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.367
It's just straight like, "Oh,
I'm getting on well

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:23.400
with Cookie Monster.
It's a bit weird that this guy

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.734
is around all the time.

00:06:24.734 --> 00:06:26.868
-Later you say to that guy,
"What's it like working

00:06:26.868 --> 00:06:29.267
with Cookie Monster everyday?"
He's like, "It's me!"

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:31.133
-No, it's not you.
It's not you.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:32.100
You seem angrier.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:37.133
-Hey, we'll be right back
with more from John Oliver.

